WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.540
- What do we do about this,

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:04.440
other than protesting and
chaining ourselves to trees?

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.270
Yes, by all means let's do that,

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:10.650
but we also need to figure
out ways where we can emulate

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.660
some of these more careful
management practices

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.950
based on being on the land and listening

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.120
and trying to protect
those wonderful resources.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.400
And all that at the same time

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.970
is protecting biodiversity,
carbon storage,

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.370
and all while climate is changing

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.550
and making it harder and
harder and harder to do that.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.930
So how can we help these forests?

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.920
So, I started a project
about five years ago

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.010
to try to address some of these things

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:39.480
called The Mother Tree Project.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.250
The basic idea is taking
that basic research

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.150
on tree communication and connection

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.820
and applying it in forestry practices

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.720
so that we can actually
look after the basic things

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.540
that we need in order to
have a life on this planet

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.770
which means protecting our forests,

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:58.470
protecting the carbon stocks
that are in those forests,

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.550
the biodiversity that's in them.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.610
And so, we wanted to retain
mother trees in different amounts

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.770
and configurations with
the idea that we knew

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.890
that they were essential to regeneration

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:11.360
and essential to protecting

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.550
all the other creatures in the forest.

00:01:13.550 --> 00:01:15.790
And we also wanted to
look at genetic gradients

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:18.030
because we knew already that the rate

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.070
of climate change is so fast,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.650
the velocity is so high
that trees cannot migrate

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.590
as quickly as we need them to in order

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.180
to retain forests on our earth,

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.270
to retain forested landscape.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.060
We're gonna have to help them migrate.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:35.040
We're gonna have to help
species and genotypes move

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.960
because we've made it too fast for them

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.200
to do it on their own.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.740
I'm gonna show you some of
our early results on this.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.860
We focused on one particular
forest type, Douglas fir,

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.480
because it's got this big, broad range

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.720
across British Columbia.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.020
So these are undergraduate
and graduate students

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.880
and they are the
lifeblood of this project.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.430
It's a hundred-year project.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.410
I'm gonna be gone well before the forest

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:57.730
reaches a teenage year,

00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.160
and so they're gonna be the ones

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.840
that are carrying this into the future.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.300
This is the biggest
experiment I've ever designed.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.660
It's probably one of the
biggest climate experiments

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.760
in forests in North America.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.100
So we have actually a total of 27 forests

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:13.000
that have been treated in different ways

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.720
where we retain mother trees
in different configurations

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.670
and compare them to an
uncut primary forest,

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:21.580
so a native forest.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.270
And then we've taken that native forest

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:25.480
and cut out different patches of trees,

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.430
leaving these different mother trees

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.110
in different amounts and configurations.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:33.400
So the 60% means that
we cut out gaps of trees

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.430
and left 60% of the mother trees

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:39.070
and thinned them from below
to try to boost their vigor.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:40.060
And then the next one,

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.040
this 30% is we created
larger gaps in the forest

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:44.950
and left patches of 30%,

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.420
so just smaller patches.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.110
This 10% is what's known
as a seed tree method.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.480
It's a very common
forest harvesting method

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.370
across Europe and North America

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.150
where you leave individual trees.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.964
The problem with it is
that what we have found

00:02:57.964 --> 00:03:00.200
across British Columbia anyway

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.490
is that about 1/3 of those trees
are dead within five years.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.800
And that's part of the
reasoning behind having

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.060
these 30 and 60% patches as well,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.180
to protect the mother trees
from dying, from blowing over,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.460
or just dying from stress

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:16.642
or shock from losing its
neighbors and its family.

00:03:16.642 --> 00:03:19.059
 

